From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 09 Oct 2026 18:59:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Coroutine kernel: every guest thread on its own host stack;
 pk_runtime library

The kernel no longer blocks by saving a context and unwinding native frames to a
dispatcher. The scheduler runs on the program's stack, each guest thread runs on a
host stack of its own (psprecomp::Coroutine), and a thread that waits, is pre-empted
or ends switches back to the scheduler from wherever it is, in the middle of a system
call, a guest function called from the system, a callback or an interrupt handler.
Threads are never unwound across stacks. docs of the contract: pk_guest_call,
pk_hle_call, pk_fatal, pk_yield.

* Scheduling follows ThreadMan: a pre-empted thread goes to the front of its priority
  queue, a woken or started one to the back, equal priorities are not time-sliced
  (<prefix>_STARVE_EQUAL=1 restores the first kernel's rotation in the spin hook).
* Interrupts run nested on the running thread, or on a coroutine of their own when no
  thread runs; they cannot wait (SCE_KERNEL_ERROR_CAN_NOT_WAIT).
* Two clocks: virtual (default, repeatable, <prefix>_CPU_NS_PER_INSN and
  <prefix>_FRAME_LIMIT_US as before) and realtime (<prefix>_CLOCK=realtime) on the
  new KernelPlatform interface (monotonic clock, wait on a signalable event, signal).
* <prefix>_CO_STACK_KB (default 256), <prefix>_SPIN_INSNS, <prefix>_SPIN_REPORT,
  <prefix>_STACK_REPORT, <prefix>_TRACE_SWITCH.
* pk_runtime: the system layer without a window, renderer, installer screens or input
  devices, as a static library (PORTABLEKIT_BUILD_RUNTIME_LIBRARY, always built with
  the tests). It links with only a profile; the tests no longer compile the host
  sources a second time.
* tests/kernel_tests.cpp: hand-assembled guest code in the interpreter, nine
  scenarios (equal priorities, pre-emption to the front of the queue, priority change,
  waiting inside a guest function called from the system, interrupts nested and while
  idle, terminate and delete, 300 short lives, a stop from three calls deep), each in
  a process of its own.

Verified: ctest 31/31 on x86-64 Linux (virtual clock). Not verified on a Vita.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

